I didn't look at the overal PR­—just at usage of `unsafe`, which occurs six times:

```rust
unsafe impl<T: std::marker::Send> std::marker::Send for Lock<T> {}
unsafe impl<T: std::marker::Send> std::marker::Sync for Lock<T> {}

unsafe impl<T: std::marker::Send> std::marker::Send for RefLock<T> {}
unsafe impl<T: std::marker::Send> std::marker::Sync for RefLock<T> {}

unsafe impl<L: SLock> std::marker::Sync for SessionGlobals<L> {}

unsafe impl<T, L: SLock> Sync for SSharded<T, L> {}
```

(By the way, `std::marker::` shouldn't be necessary. `Sync` and `Send` are part of the prelude.)

- `Send` and `Sync` for `Lock<T>`:
  Why are these here? It just wraps a regular Mutex<T>, which already has these Send and Sync implementations.

- `Send` for `RefLock<T>`:
  Same as above. RefCell already has this Send implementation.

- `Sync` for `RefLock<T>`:
  :warning: This seems very wrong. This type wraps a RefCell without any additional locking mechanism, which is definitely not Sync.

- Sync for `SessionGlobals<L>` and `SSharded<L>`:
  These types only contain only locks (and symbol::Interner), which all already should be Sync. 

  Either all fields are already Sync and this is sound but unnecessary, or some fields are not Sync and this is actually unsound.

